[Music]  hi everyone i'm sophia product manager  from google cloud i'm here today to tell  you about cloud asset inventory  well i'll know by bringing workload to  cloud you're benefiting from its  scalability high performance secureness  and cost efficiency  yet at the same time admins especially  platform admins secure elements are  facing never before seen challenges they  were brought in by the large  quantitative resources and data that can  be easily created by developers  in fact enterprises have millions of  resources running on google cloud today  so how can you see all the things you  have on cloud at the same time how can  you keep an eye on all the changes as  they happen so you don't miss a threat  how do you answer questions that seem  simple but actually challenging like  how many vms do i have in my org  who can change my billing accounts  or what service account keys that are  older than 60 days  i'm very excited to introduce your cloud  assign inventory that aim to solve all  these challenges for you as you can see  here cloud assign inventory is a fully  managed metadata service  so let's first address the elephant in  the room what is an asset  an asset can be a resource like a vm  instance a bigquery table  it can also be a policy like impolicy or  policy  it can also be vm random information  like system updates  so now let's talk about metadata  metadata here means the configuration of  the asset not the actual customer  content  for example it is about the name the  size creation time for the disk but not  the files that are written on the disks  it offers many even trade services on  your entire gcp and enthalpy environment  which makes it truly a one-stop shop  so you don't need to go to computer  engine to find all your vms and then go  to cloud storage to see all your buckets  for example  and what is more convenient than that it  works at the organization folder and the  project level with find green and policy  controls so regardless of your  permission level you can always take a  full advantage of it  lastly you can get up to 35 days of full  change history from cloud asset  inventory  now let's dive deeper into the features  first and foremost you can get a  snapshot of your entire inventory you  can use either the export or the lisa  service  the difference is that  export is a long-running operation which  will export your data to either a cloud  storage bucket or a bigquery table which  makes it easier for you to directly  query and analyze data later  this api works just like any other lease  api except it supports a timestamp  where all the data will be included in  the standard http response in case you  don't want to deal with a storage  location you can also get a full change  history by either using the ui or api  which i'll demo later  now after getting full visibility for  your entire inventory plus history  we also offer real time notification  feature for you to easily achieve  continuous monitoring  it allows you to monitor assets and will  send a pops-up message when change  occurs  you can define your criterias by as a  name as a type or by org folder and  project you can even use cell condition  and by the way cell is expression  language that created by google  for example you can say notify me as  soon as any file rules got changed in my  org  or notify me only if my policy is  changed to be open or when a vm's  deleted  here's an example scenario an admin  wants to monitor and get notified when a  gmail account is added to an end policy  you'll first define your condition in a  real-time feed using the code shown  below  now if a malicious user adds his own  gmail account into the policy cloud  asset inventory will trigger cloud pops  up message right to you in real time  from there you can define your own  remediation actions for example you can  write a cloud function to reorder back  the change or send an email to project  owners the real-time monitoring  capability especially the conditions  supported through cell ensures that you  won't miss any important changes and  also filters out the noises so you can  stay focused and keep your costs low  okay now if you're one of the policy  admins or privacy admins who have to be  on top of access grants take care of  access certificate and keep track of all  the changes across your whole org you  will be really excited to learn a  powerful tool offered about cloud asset  inventory i am policy analyzer  in a nutshell it does deep and thorough  analysis on policies and related  resources to help you answer the  question who can access what and why  for example what service accounts can  access my buckets that contain sensitive  data or what access does this terminated  employee still has  so what makes the tool powerful let me  show you with the actual example  who can change my firewall rules  this is one of the top questions adam  need to answer and keep track to  well turns out you have to peel through  many layers to actually get to the full  picture  starting from the whole part  who can be a user a group or even a  service account  in the case of group there can be  multiple layers of groups which we call  nessus groups in case of a service  account there can also be multiple  layers of service accounts that can  impersonate one another  policy analyzer can handle all of the  above to give you a complete picture on  the whole part  now let's look at what's needed for  changing a variable rule  turns out three permissions create  update or delete can all make changes to  firewalls in some ways  policy analyzer is capable of mapping  item rules to permissions for both  security rules and custom rules  and last let's look at the last part of  the question project a  we all know iom policies can be  inherited which requires you to not only  look at and policy directly on project a  here but also trace up along the  hierarchy to the folders and the orgs  above to make sure all the policies are  analyzed  luckily policy analyzer analyzes  resource and the policy hierarchy as  well  it also analyzes im conditions and  exports results to cloud storage buckets  or bitcoin table  so here you go you can see how much work  the policy analyzer does for you behind  the scene in order to provide a truly  complete and comprehensive answer on who  can access what and why  now it's the demo time as i mentioned  earlier let's take a quick look at the  cloud asset inventory developer console  the cloud asset inventory developer  console is located under the iem and  admin tab first you can use the toggle  on the top to switch between org folder  or project that would like to see your  asset set  once landed on the landing page you will  see an overview tab with automatically  pre-populated dashboards for your  deployment insights also resource and  and policy tabs for details about assets  the first geograph shows you how your  zonal and original resources are located  across the globe you can also get to a  table view of it  the next one shows how your vm instance  accounts have been changing over time  and the next field shows you the  resource console-based services and  asset types  you can also use the predefined filters  on the left to focus only on assets  you're interested in like vm instances  you can see i have 17 and they all live  in the u.s central region  click anywhere on the dashboard it will  take you to the corresponding resource  and policy tab you can see here all the  field options are carried over from the  graph setting before so let's uncheck  them  let's assume i want to search for  certain vm instances so i can first  check an asset type here and then i will  further refine my search results by  typing the search term into the search  bar like name contains the instance and  then label key contains color group  and here are my results  i can also search by directly typing in  the certain asset attribute values like  unknown external ip as that's what i  found on the internet i want to locate  which vm it is  and here you go i found my vm  i can then click on the instance to see  the detail page you can see the high  level summary first  also if compute engine vm manager is  enabled you can see the runtime  information as well like os version  kernel running or stopped  you can go to the change history tab to  see the full change history up to 35  days  you can see here all the time stamps  shows the ones that had a metadata  change  and i can choose two time stamps to  compare the differences  in this case i can see that a label  color group pink was removed between the  two versions  as well as some other change  next you can go to the full metadata  type for all the details like a  description disk service account etc  and lastly you can see the m policy  that's attached to the resource in the  impolicy tab  that is all for today and we encourage  all of you to try it out  check out the documentation product  pages down below and we're looking  forward to hearing from all of you thank  you very much for watching  [Music]  you

